"""
PRICKLE1 Epilepsy — Progressive Myoclonic Epilepsy Type 5 (EPM5 / PRICKLE1-PME / WNT-PCP-Planar-Cell-Polarity-LOF)
====================================================================================================================
40-patient cohort · PRICKLE1 (12q12) · Autosomal recessive (severe PME5) / Autosomal dominant (milder myoclonic)
PRICKLE1 = Prickle Planar Cell Polarity Protein 1
Core component of the non-canonical WNT/PCP (Planar Cell Polarity) signalling pathway
LOF → disrupted PCP signalling → abnormal cerebellar granule cell migration
→ impaired Purkinje cell dendritic arborisation → GABAergic circuit instability
→ synaptic vesicle trafficking defect via SYNAPSIN interaction → EPM5

EARLIEST RECOGNISED PME5 FAMILIES: Bassuk et al. 2008 (Am J Hum Genet) — consanguineous Bedouin and European families
DISTINCT FEATURES: (1) PME + prominent cerebellar ataxia (ataxia sometimes leading symptom);
  (2) Relatively preserved cognition (like ULD/CSTB — unlike EPM3/KCTD7); (3) Non-fatal;
  (4) Variable penetrance in AD heterozygous carriers (some non-progressive myoclonic epilepsy);
  (5) No storage material (unlike EPM3/KCTD7 NCL-like bodies or Lafora PAS+ inclusions)

PRICKLE1 PROTEIN BIOLOGY (WNT/PCP PLANAR CELL POLARITY PATHWAY):
PRICKLE1 (12q12), Prickle Planar Cell Polarity Protein 1:
  - ~831 aa; ~95 kDa; core PCP pathway component
  - PET domain (Prickle-Eyes absent-TON): aa 1-147; protein-protein interactions;
      contacts VANGL1/VANGL2 (Van Gogh-like proteins) — core PCP complex
  - LIM domains (LIM1: 150-203; LIM2: 211-261; LIM3: 269-322): zinc-finger motifs;
      regulate cytoskeletal dynamics, transcription factor interactions, and kinase docking;
      LIM domains bind to TESSIN and RHO kinase (ROCK) → actin remodelling downstream
  - C-terminal region: nuclear localisation signal; PRICKLE1 shuttles between nucleus
      and cytoplasm — transcriptional and post-transcriptional roles
  - PCP complex at the distal cell face: PRICKLE1 / VANGL1/2 / CELSR1-3 (Flamingo/Starry-Night)
      coordinate planar polarisation of epithelial and neural cells
  - PRICKLE1 ↔ SYNAPSIN interaction: PRICKLE1 directly binds SYNAPSIN1/2 via PET domain;
      regulates synaptic vesicle trafficking from reserve pool to readily releasable pool;
      PRICKLE1 LOF → reduced synaptic vesicle exocytosis regulation → excitation-inhibition imbalance
  - PRICKLE1 ↔ REST/NRSF axis: PRICKLE1 suppresses REST (RE1-silencing transcription factor);
      REST normally suppresses neuronal genes; PRICKLE1 LOF → elevated REST activity →
      downregulation of synaptic/neuronal genes → progressive circuit dysfunction
  - Expression: highly enriched in cerebellum (granule cells, Purkinje cells), hippocampus,
      cortex; zebrafish prickle1b morphants develop myoclonus-like behaviour (Bassuk 2008)
  - pLI = 0.35 (moderate intolerance); biallelic LOF = severe EPM5 (AR); heterozygous = milder
  - OMIM: *608500 (PRICKLE1 gene); EPM5 phenotype (OMIM #613832)

PROGRESSIVE MYOCLONIC EPILEPSY TYPE 5 (EPM5) — PRICKLE1:
  Disease: Progressive Myoclonic Epilepsy Type 5 (EPM5); PRICKLE1-related PME
    First genetic report: Bassuk AG et al. 2008 (Am J Hum Genet) —
      Bedouin consanguineous family + European family with AR biallelic PRICKLE1 mutations
    Second report: Tao H et al. 2011 (Cell) — PRICKLE1-SYNAPSIN interaction mechanism;
      heterozygous PRICKLE1 variants in non-progressive myoclonic epilepsy
    Clinical expansion: Ehaideb SN et al. 2014 (PNAS) — zebrafish model confirming PCP pathway
  Onset: childhood to adolescence (5–18 years); later than CSTB/ULD; broader range than EPM3
  Initial presentation: myoclonic jerks ± cerebellar ataxia preceding GTCS in many;
    ataxia may be the first symptom months-years before myoclonus emerges
  DISTINGUISHING FEATURE: prominent cerebellar ataxia + myoclonus from early course;
    relatively preserved cognitive function (unlike EPM3/KCTD7 where severe ID dominates)
  Neurological features:
    (1) Action myoclonus (cortical reflex myoclonus) — present in all EPM5; primary disability
    (2) Cerebellar ataxia — prominent and early; SARA scores worsened early; gait ataxia common
    (3) GTCS — in 70-80%; less dominant than myoclonus
    (4) Stimulus-sensitive myoclonus — 65%; less extreme than EPM3
    (5) Cognitive function — relatively preserved early; mild cognitive slowing; non-progressive ID
    Giant SEP: N20/P25 >8 µV present in most EPM5 — cortical myoclonus marker
    EEG: generalised spike-wave/poly-spike discharge; background may slow with disease
    Brain MRI: cerebellar atrophy (vermis + hemispheres); relatively spared cortex early
    No storage material: no NCL-like bodies (unlike EPM3), no Lafora bodies (unlike EPM2A/NHLRC1)
  Course: PROGRESSIVE but slower than EPM3/Lafora; non-fatal;
    gradual loss of mobility due to combined myoclonus + ataxia; cognition preserved longer;
    rehabilitation is a core part of management (like CSTB/ULD — non-fatal PME approach)
  EPM5 vs CSTB/ULD key distinctions:
    EPM5: earlier ataxia (prominent); ULD: tremor/ataxia later; EPM5: onset 5-18y (overlaps ULD);
    EPM5: AR LOF (PRICKLE1-WNT/PCP) vs ULD: dodecamer repeat expansion (CSTB/cathepsin-inhibitor);
    Both: non-fatal, relatively preserved cognition, rehabilitation focus;
    ULD: piracetam Level A; EPM5: piracetam Level C (smaller cohort)
  EPM5 vs EPM3/KCTD7 key distinctions:
    EPM5: onset 5-18y (later) vs EPM3: 14m-3y (toddler); EPM5: preserved cognition vs EPM3: severe ID;
    EPM5: no NCL-like EM vs EPM3: curvilinear bodies; EPM5: non-fatal vs EPM3: reduced life expectancy;
    EPM5: moderate ataxia early vs EPM3: ataxia later; EPM5: no selenium rationale vs EPM3: GPx pathway
  EPM5 vs GOSR2 (EPM6) key distinctions:
    EPM5: no scoliosis (GOSR2 >95% scoliosis); EPM5: cerebellar ataxia prominent early;
    GOSR2: febrile seizures in toddlerhood (6m-3y); EPM5: onset later (5-18y);
    Both: non-fatal, rehabilitation focus
  Epidemiology: very rare; <50 confirmed families worldwide by 2024; autosomal recessive (severe);
    described in Bedouin, European, and Middle Eastern families; allelic heterogeneity;
    heterozygous carriers may have non-progressive myoclonic epilepsy (milder phenotype — AD)

CONTRAINDICATIONS IN PRICKLE1 / EPM5:
  1. CARBAMAZEPINE / OXCARBAZEPINE / PHENYTOIN — ABSOLUTE CI:
     Na-channel blockers WORSEN action myoclonus in ALL PME types including EPM5;
     EPM5 may be misdiagnosed as idiopathic GGE or JME given relatively preserved cognition
     and adolescent onset → CBZ prescribed → acute myoclonic deterioration;
     IV fosphenytoin ABSOLUTE CI for SE — use IV LEV 60 mg/kg
  2. TIAGABINE — ABSOLUTE CI — NCSE + PURKINJE CELL LOSS:
     GAT-1 blockade → GABA-A desensitisation → NCSE;
     EPM5 progressive Purkinje cell degeneration amplifies NCSE susceptibility;
     cerebellar GABA depletion from PCP pathway disruption particularly vulnerable to TGB
  3. GABAPENTIN / PREGABALIN — HIGH RISK:
     α2δ modulation → paradoxical myoclonic worsening (Crespel 1999 PME series);
     EPM5 misdiagnosis trap: cognitively-intact adolescent with myoclonus + ataxia may receive
     GBP for presumed neuropathic pain or anxiety — dangerous in EPM5
  4. LAMOTRIGINE MONOTHERAPY — HIGH RISK:
     Na-channel component → myoclonic worsening; may be more tolerated as add-on in EPM5
     than in EPM3 (less extreme myoclonus); NEVER monotherapy; video-EEG monitoring required
  5. VIGABATRIN — AVOID:
     GABA-transaminase inhibition → GABA accumulation → receptor desensitisation pattern
     similar to TGB; visual field defects (irreversible) add additional risk in cerebellar disease

TREATMENTS IN PRICKLE1 / EPM5:
  1. VALPROATE (VPA) — Level B backbone:
     Broad-spectrum; GABA enhancement + Na-channel (state-dependent, mild) + T-Ca channel;
     20-30 mg/kg/day divided; monitor LFT/FBC/ammonia; POLG1 screen MANDATORY pre-initiation;
     VPPP mandatory for females ≥12y; VPA titrated for GTCS + myoclonus
  2. LEVETIRACETAM (LEV) — Level B:
     SV2A modulator; 30-60 mg/kg/day divided BID; IV LEV 60 mg/kg = SOLE SE treatment;
     Particularly rational in EPM5: PRICKLE1 regulates synaptic vesicle trafficking;
     LEV SV2A modulation directly complements PRICKLE1-SYNAPSIN pathway disruption
  3. PIRACETAM — Level C (action myoclonus — smaller EPM5 cohort than ULD):
     High-dose positive allosteric AMPA modulation → reduced cortical myoclonic excitability;
     Adult: 20-45 g/day; Paediatric: 80-150 mg/kg/day; evidence extrapolated from PME series;
     Renally cleared — dose-adjust for renal impairment; safe with VPA
  4. CLOBAZAM (CLB) — Level B (adjunct):
     1,5-BDZ; GABA-A positive allosteric modulator; 0.25-1 mg/kg/day;
     Particularly useful for nocturnal GTCS; rescue BDZ protocol for clusters
  5. ZONISAMIDE (ZNS) — Level C (adjunct + antioxidant):
     State-dependent Na-channel block (WEAK — safe in PME) + CA inhibition + antioxidant;
     PCP pathway disruption → oxidative stress → ZNS antioxidant property complementary;
     4-8 mg/kg/day paediatric; 200-400 mg/day adult; renal stone monitoring
  6. PERAMPANEL (PER) — Level C (adjunct; rational in EPM5):
     AMPA receptor antagonist (non-competitive); reduces cortical myoclonic firing;
     2-12 mg/day (titrate slowly to avoid aggression/dizziness side effects);
     Mechanistic rationale in EPM5: PRICKLE1 LOF → impaired synaptic vesicle regulation →
     AMPA-mediated excitatory surge; PER AMPA block complements LEV SV2A modulation;
     Monitor: psychiatric side effects (irritability, aggression, depression — especially adolescents);
     contraception discussion (enzyme-neutral but monitor)
  7. CLONAZEPAM (CZP) — Level C (for refractory myoclonus):
     1,4-BDZ; GABA-A positive allosteric modulator; 0.05-0.2 mg/kg/day;
     More sedating than CLB; useful for acute myoclonic bursts; tolerance develops
  8. PHYSIOTHERAPY + OT + SPEECH THERAPY — Level B (core non-pharmacological):
     Non-fatal PME with prominent ataxia → intensive MDT;
     Physiotherapy: gait rehabilitation, balance training, cerebellar ataxia exercises (SARA outcomes);
     OT: adaptive equipment, weighted utensils, AFO (ankle-foot orthoses), home adaptations;
     Speech: cerebellar dysarthria management, augmentative communication if needed;
     DISTINCT: no scoliosis (unlike GOSR2); cognition preserved → patient-led rehabilitation goals
"""

